{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1540509-hyperbolic-yu-shan-theory-or-how-do-the-gods-commute-to-work","id":1540509,"title":"Hyperbolic Yu-Shan theory (Or, how do the gods commute to work?)","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Exalted"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-08-16T08:35:19-07:00","id":1540509,"title":"Hyperbolic Yu-Shan theory (Or, how do the gods commute to work?)","content":"Yu-Shan is &quot;a city the size of a continent&quot;. What does this mean? Cities in real life are defined by the fact that it's possible to get from one part of the city to another in a reasonable amount of time. In particular, many people live in one part of the city and work in another. Various types of entertainment and cultural events also bring people from different parts of the city to one place. This interconnectedness influences everything about cities. For example it's very common to have the best jobs and events concentrated in the city center, with the land closest to it consequently the most valuable, and land further and further away progressively cheaper. If a city is not interconnected, it would more likely be described as multiple cities and towns. <br /> <br /> Yu-Shan is apparently not interconnected very well, being the size of a continent, but with most transit options used sounding like they are slower than modern cars and trains. (We know from the book that walking is the most &quot;ubiquitous&quot; form of transit, with the canals second-most. Dragon boats seem like the most common watercraft. In our world these are racing rowboats, so they go pretty fast for a rowboat but slow compared to a motorboat or car. Other vehicles include &quot;pleasure barges&quot; which don't sound very fast, or very pleasurable if other vehicles are racing by at high speed. The dragon line rail is pretty fast, but can't be relied on as there's only one train and it crosses Yu-Shan in a few days so the frequencey it runs at should be very low.) So how does this work? Is it really one city? How do the gods commute to work?<br /> <br /> I think these questions are actually pretty important to telling stories set in Yu-Shan. Understanding this stuff should inform the appearance of different districts in Yu-Shan and how characters get around. A big theme in the Sidereal book is class distinctions among different gods. In cities, your house is a big part of class distinctions - how big it is and where it is. This should especially be true for goods in Yu-Shan where food is a luxury, not a necessity, so housing should be one of the biggest expenses. So understanding where people live and why can be important to telling stories about the troubles of unemployed gods, corruption, and class conflict. Some theories I have heard, and one I came up with:<br /> <br /> <i>Polycentric Yu-Shan theory</i>: Most gods live a short distance from their workplace, and different workplaces are scattered around the city of Yu-Shan. A disinterested outside observer would consider Yu-Shan a continent containing multiple cities, centered around different departments of the Celestial Bureaucracy or important locations for inter-Yu-Shan trade. But the gods say it's one city and they're in charge so it's one city. Some kind of fast communication allows the different departments in different places to work together reasonably well.<br /> <br /> <i>Fast Travel Theory</i>: The travel options available in Yu-Shan are actually faster than they seem. It's possible to cross a good portion of the continent in a few hours. The structure of Yu-Shan is like an ordinary ancient or modern city, but blown up by a huge factor: All the houses are larger, the city center is larger, and outlying areas are further from the city center. Dragon boats are, I guess, as fast as modern cars? Faster? And other options are faster still.<br /> <br /> <i>Time Dilation Theory</i>: Since gods don't actually need to sleep, they don't regularly work during the day and then go home to their houses at night. Instead, they return to their houses for longer breaks. A long boat ride isn't unpleasant for them, so the average poor god lives a full day's travel away from their house, maybe more. A god's house is in some ways more like what we would consider to be a vacation home. Humans working at the Celestial Bureaucracy, like Sidereals, have very different rythms, living much closer and coming to work every day. Especially when combined with fast travel theory, this could render the whole continent usable.<br /> <br /> <i>Hyperbolic Yu-Shan Theory</i>: This is my personal favorite. Space and distance work differently in Yu-Shan than they do in Creation, and specifically follow the rules of hyperbolic space. This means a small change in direction leads to a big distanct between your final destination and your original destination. This means more area can be reached within the same distance from a fixed point. If space is curved enough, it is totally possible for an entire continent to be within, say, a 3-hour boat ride of the city center (along a highly branching network of canals). Hell is a sphere and Creation is flat, so why shouldn't Heaven by hyperbolic? This does contradict one line in the book, about the dragon line rail taking days to get from one side of Yu-Shan to the other, unless it takes a very circuitous route (but maybe that's fine).<br /> <br /> So what do you think? Do you agree with one of these theories, or believe another?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25834-habitableexoplanet","name":"habitableexoplanet","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"296"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-16T10:40:24-07:00","id":1540515,"title":"","content":"I think in the absence of being given precise figures, one ought to read such passages in the context of how all of the description of travel is following the comment of &quot;travelling this gigantic city would be an epic undertaking were it not for the wonders of Heaven&quot;.<br /> <br /> Dragon boats are certainly something I'd take in the spirit of &quot;this is a setting in which there are literal dragons (one of whom is given the stats of being able to fly at a speed of 500 miles per hour in the very same book)&quot; .<br /> <br /> And I do think it would make sense for gods to work in shifts different to what might align with human circadian rhythms. Not just because they don't need to sleep, but because Yu-Shan doesn't even have proper day and night. Sure, it tracks the passage of time via the Golden Barque, but the actual sky and ambient light levels are solely a function of which Incarna is leading in the Games of Divinity.<br /> <br /> I don't recall if it made it into Charting Fate's Course, but prior Editions placed that alongside the way food and general health work to make the mortal experience of time in Yu-Shan rather weird. Not for something as sci-fi as time dilation, rather the disorientation of having familiar frames of reference for the passage of time removed at the same time as their physical needs are placed into a greater experience of ease.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-18T04:26:05-07:00","id":1540614,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1540509-hyperbolic-yu-shan-theory-or-how-do-the-gods-commute-to-work?p=1540515#post1540515\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think in the absence of being given precise figures, one ought to read such passages in the context of how all of the description of travel is following the comment of &quot;travelling this gigantic city would be an epic undertaking were it not for the wonders of Heaven&quot;.<br /> <br /> Dragon boats are certainly something I'd take in the spirit of &quot;this is a setting in which there are literal dragons (one of whom is given the stats of being able to fly at a speed of 500 miles per hour in the very same book)&quot; .<br /> <br /> And I do think it would make sense for gods to work in shifts different to what might align with human circadian rhythms. Not just because they don't need to sleep, but because Yu-Shan doesn't even have proper day and night. Sure, it tracks the passage of time via the Golden Barque, but the actual sky and ambient light levels are solely a function of which Incarna is leading in the Games of Divinity.<br /> <br /> I don't recall if it made it into Charting Fate's Course, but prior Editions placed that alongside the way food and general health work to make the mortal experience of time in Yu-Shan rather weird. Not for something as sci-fi as time dilation, rather the disorientation of having familiar frames of reference for the passage of time removed at the same time as their physical needs are placed into a greater experience of ease.</div> </div> </div> </div>Good point. The exact quote is<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Traversing Yu-Shan\u2019s massive sprawl would be an epic undertaking were it not for its wonders of transportation. Overseen by the Department of Celestial Concerns, these marvels let the wealthy and powerful travel in speed and splendor, whether walking curated park-lined avenues, skimming quicksilver canals, or soaring on wings of flame. </div> </div> </div><br /> So this implies that there is a big difference between how easily the rich and powerful are able to travel and how easily others can.<br /> <br /> <br /> I like your idea of starting with the speed of dragons. So the smallest continent in our world is Australia, which is 3 million square miles. It's reasonable to assume Yu-Shan is roughly the same size. A perfect circle with a radius of 1000 miles would be about the same area. We know Yu-Shan isn't a perfect circle, but probably most of it is within 1000 miles of the city center. So to a dragon that can travel at 500 miles per hour, it feels roughly like a modern-day city, with the furthest reaches a two-hour commute away. Anyone with access to transit as fast as a dragon will feel the same way.<br /> <br /> <br /> If someone can travel at Stormwind Rider speeds, of 100 miles per hour, its 10 hours to get far from the city center. So most neighborhoods in the city only make sense to live in if they have an inhuman schedule where home is basically a vacation. 5 - 10 hours is not so bad as the total length of a trip to a weeklong vacation, especially if the exhaustion, hunger, and discomfort that occur on trips for humans are not a problem for you.<br /> <br /> <br /> The biggest problem I had with the &quot;frequent weeklong vacations&quot; theory of Yu-Shan schedules is that you kind of have to ignore it whenever the PCs need to talk to a particular god, though I just realized it's probably fun for PCs to try to find them at their house.<br /> <br /> <br /> If a lot of rich gods can travel at the speed of dragons, or at least Stormwind Riders, but poor gods' options are slower than that, you get a situation where the city feels like one city only to the rich, with each neighborhood a city in itself to the poor. Rich gods move quickly between work and recreation centers in different parts of the continent, while a poor god has to choose one (and can probably only live near the recreation center if they work there).<br /> <br /> <br /> But that still leaves the question of what speed poor gods travel at. The dragon boat does seem like it should be the main form of public transportation of the lowest-level workers in the Celestial Bureaucracy. Dragon boats in real life have a top speed of 12.5 mph. On the other hand, dragons have a top speed of 500 mph. So it\u2019s somewhere in between there. If they\u2019re willing to take a 24 hour commute then 12.5 mph gives a 300 mile effective radius, which isn\u2019t that bad.<br /> <br /> <br /> One weird indicator I looked at comes from skimming boats. I figured that boats that skim the water have some minimum speed where it makes sense to use them instead of ordinary boats and some maximum speed where it makes sense to use them instead of flying, and these shouldn\u2019t change too much in Yu-Shan vs. our world (uh, ignoring that it\u2019s mercury instead of water). The first commercially viable hydrofoils went at 40 mph and modern designs can travel at 180 mph, so it\u2019s a big range but the skimming boats should be somewhere in there. But these are an option for the wealthy, so the average citizen is probably traveling at a lesser speed than that. But that still leaves a wide variation.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25834-habitableexoplanet","name":"habitableexoplanet","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"296"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}